Let's take the scenario of a parent component with a state and pass this state and a function to set this state as props to its children. The children can then use these to set the state as per their need.

Now, this can become a burden to the parent component as it has to maintain the state of every child. To avoid this, let's use React Context for this use-case.

The parent component will contain the Context, and the child components will use this context to set their states.

First, let's create a context that will wrap the entire app.

// TooltipContext.js
import React from 'react';

const TooltipContext = React.createContext();

export default TooltipContext;

Now, let's create a component to wrap the app and pass the state and setter function as values to this context.

// TooltipProvider.js
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import TooltipContext from './TooltipContext';

This is the component that we'll use in our app. It can be anywhere in the app.

// App.js
import React from 'react';
import TooltipContext from './TooltipContext';

export default function App() {
return (
<>
<TooltipProvider>
{/* All your components here */}
</TooltipProvider>
</>
);
}

Next, let's create the tooltip component that will be rendered when we click on something.

This component will receive a function that sets the state in the context and the id of the current tooltip.

// Tooltip.js
import React from 'react';
import TooltipContext from './TooltipContext';

Let's use this component whereever we want. For example, we can create a button that will show this tooltip.

// ShowTooltip.js
import React from 'react';
import TooltipContext from './TooltipContext';

export default function ShowTooltip({ children }) {
return (
<TooltipContext.Consumer>
{({ setTooltip }) => (
<button onClick={() => setTooltip(children)}>
{children}
</button>
)}
</TooltipContext.Consumer>
);
}

This component will use the context to get the state set function and pass it to the onClick event handler. Now, whenever we click on this button, it will open the tooltip and close the previously opened tooltip.

